{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Let there be luminaries</b> — Behold, the light was created on the first day and illuminated in the elements, and when the firmament was made on the second, it separated in the light and prevented it from illuminating in the lower elements. And behold, when the earth was created on the third day, there was darkness upon it and no light. And now on the fourth day, HaKadosh Barukh Hu desired that there be luminaries in the firmament to bring light to the earth. And this is the meaning of \"in the firmament of the heavens to illuminate upon the earth,\" for the light had been above the firmament and did not illuminate upon the earth. And the sense of \"let there be luminaries\" is that from the matter of the heavens He decreed at first that there be light in the measure of day, and now He decreed that it become embodied and that there come into being from it a body that illuminates by day — the great luminary — and another body — the small luminary — that illuminates by night, and that both be suspended in the firmament of the heavens so that they illuminate also below. And it is possible that just as He placed in the earth the power of vegetation in certain places within it, so He placed in the firmament prepared and designated places to receive the illumination, and these bodies are receivers of brilliant light, such as theaspaklariot (crystalline mirrors) and the shoham stones; therefore He calls them מְאוֹרוֹת (luminaries), not אוֹרִים (lights), even though the Psalmist called them thus (Psalms 136:7)." "<b>To distinguish between the day and between the night</b> — Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 1:14): from the time the first light was stored away; but during the six days of Creation the light and the darkness served, this by day and this by night. And I do not see that this is the opinion of our Sages who mention \"storage\" concerning the first light; rather, according to them the first light served for three days, and on the fourth it was emanated from and the two luminaries were made from it at that time, as they said (Genesis Rabbah 17:5): the splendor of the light that was above became the orb of the sun. For since this world was not fit to use that light without intermediation, He stored it away for the righteous in the world to come, and they used its splendor from the fourth day onward. Thus they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 3:6): They taught — the light that was created during the six days of Creation: to illuminate by day it cannot, because it overpowers the orb of the sun; by night it cannot, for it was not created to illuminate except by day. And where is it? It was stored away. And where is it? It is prepared for the righteous for the future to come, as it is stated (Isaiah 30:26): \"And the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun, and the light of the sun shall be sevenfold, as the light of the seven days.\" Seven — one marvels! Were they not three? It is like a man who says, \"Thus I set aside meat for the seven days of my feast\" — meaning, it is the language of people that one says, \"I set aside and keep this meat for the seven days of my feast,\" not that it suffices for all seven, but that he will bring it out among them. And thus they said \"the seven days\" — like the light that existed during those days, in part. And there they further said (Genesis Rabbah 3:6): \"And He separated\" — Rabbi Yehudah son of Rabbi Simon said: He separated it for Himself, and the Rabbis say: He separated it for the righteous for the future to come. And if you can understand their intent in saying in the Blessing of the Moon \"a crown of splendor for those borne in the womb,\" you will know the secret of the first light and the storage and the separation that He said — \"He separated it for Himself\" — and the secret of the two kings who use one crown, as in the end the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun, after the light of the sun has become sevenfold." "<b>And they shall be for signs</b> — The change that they produce and from which signs and wonders are made in the heavens and the earth: blood and fire and pillars of smoke (Joel 3:3), as in the phrase \"and from the signs of the heavens do not be dismayed\" (Jeremiah 10:2)." "<b>And for appointed seasons</b> — Seed and harvest and cold and heat and summer and winter (Genesis 8:22)." "<b>And for days</b> — The measure of day and the measure of night." "<b>And years</b> — They shall complete their course, and resume again to travel on the path upon which they went. And the solar year is in 365 days, and the lunar year is in thirty days."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The word הקב\"ה is rendered as HaKadosh Barukh Hu per standard convention; Ramban uses this full form rather than the abbreviated form."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:13:59.014271275Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}